      "body": "ACK baf93e8ffd8430bbee99246a0bcaf8e529290e08\n\nReviewed the HTTP, P2P, and I2P paths and they look OK.\n\nThe option is set before the socket is handed to the event loop. If setting it fails, the connection is rejected, and ownership remains safe on every return path.\n\nThis change removes reliance on inheritance (which doesn't work on Linux as explained) or per-call `MSG_DONTWAIT`, and is therefore a worthwhile defense for our socket threads.\n\nThere are no tests here (nor would I expect there to be), so I had a clanker write up a small Python socket probe for Linux to verify (my) kernel's behaviour against what we are relying on here:\n\n1. A socket accepted from a nonblocking listener did not inherit `O_NONBLOCK`.\n2. `recv(..., MSG_DONTWAIT)` on that otherwise-blocking socket raised `BlockingIOError` instead of blocking.\n3. Setting `O_NONBLOCK` with `F_GETFL`/`F_SETFL` succeeded and the flag could be read back.\n4. After setting it, plain `recv(..., 0)` raised `BlockingIOError` instead of blocking.\n\nAll behaved as expected.",
      "user": {
